{
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**The problem.** A **peak** is a value strictly bigger than its neighbours. Given an array `nums` where no two neighbours are equal, return the index of **any** peak. Imagine `nums[-1]` and `nums[n]` are **−∞**, so a value at either end only needs to beat its one neighbour. Your algorithm must run in **O(log n)** time."
    },
    {
      "type": "drawing",
      "src": "/drawings/1cd1f6af-c0b3-45c7-873b-5a4f2139a543.svg",
      "alt": "1, 2, 1, 3, 5, 6, 4 drawn as bars. There are two peaks: 2 at index 1 and 6 at index 5; either answer is accepted. Binary search checks index 3: 3 is less than its right neighbour 5, so climbing right must reach a peak. Then index 5: 6 is more than 4, so a peak is at 5 or to its left. Then index 4: 5 is less than 6, so go right. Only index 5 is left.",
      "width": 876,
      "height": 456,
