    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Example"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "text",
      "code": "nums = [1, 2, 3, 1]           ->  2\nnums = [1, 2, 1, 3, 5, 6, 4]  ->  5   (or 1)\nnums = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50]   ->  4   (always rising: the last value is a peak)"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The idea: walk uphill"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Stand at `mid` and look right. If `nums[mid] < nums[mid + 1]`, the ground rises to the right. Keep walking right: either it keeps rising until the end — and the last value is a peak, because `nums[n]` is −∞ — or it falls somewhere, and the top before the fall is a peak. Either way **a peak exists to the right**, so drop the left half. If instead `nums[mid] > nums[mid + 1]`, the same argument walking left says a peak is at `mid` or to its left. The array isn't sorted and has many peaks, but each comparison still safely throws away half."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "1. Linear scan"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function findPeakElement(nums) {\n  for (let i = 0; i + 1 < nums.length; i++) {\n    if (nums[i] > nums[i + 1]) return i;   // the first downhill step: nums[i] beat both neighbours\n  }\n  return nums.length - 1;                  // always rising: the last value is a peak\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def find_peak_element(nums):\n    for i in range(len(nums) - 1):\n        if nums[i] > nums[i + 1]:\n            return i                      # the first downhill step: nums[i] beat both neighbours\n    return len(nums) - 1                  # always rising: the last value is a peak"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "int findPeakElement(int[] nums) {\n    for (int i = 0; i + 1 < nums.length; i++) {\n        if (nums[i] > nums[i + 1]) return i;   // the first downhill step: nums[i] beat both neighbours\n    }\n    return nums.length - 1;                    // always rising: the last value is a peak\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "int findPeakElement(vector<int>& nums) {\n    for (int i = 0; i + 1 < (int)nums.size(); i++) {\n        if (nums[i] > nums[i + 1]) return i;   // the first downhill step: nums[i] beat both neighbours\n    }\n    return nums.size() - 1;                    // always rising: the last value is a peak\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(n), space O(1).** Why is the first downhill step a peak? Everything before it was rising, so `nums[i]` is also bigger than `nums[i - 1]`."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "2. Binary search toward the higher side"
    },
    {
      "type": "simulator",
      "program": "peak-element",
      "caption": "Compare mid with its right neighbour and move toward the higher side."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function findPeakElement(nums) {\n  let low = 0;\n  let high = nums.length - 1;\n  while (low < high) {\n    const mid = Math.floor((low + high) / 2);\n    if (nums[mid] > nums[mid + 1]) {\n      high = mid;\n    } else {\n      low = mid + 1;\n    }\n  }\n  return low;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def find_peak_element(nums):\n    low, high = 0, len(nums) - 1\n    while low < high:\n        mid = (low + high) // 2\n        if nums[mid] > nums[mid + 1]:\n            high = mid                    # downhill: a peak is at mid or to its left\n        else:\n            low = mid + 1                 # uphill: a peak is to the right\n    return low"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "int findPeakElement(int[] nums) {\n    int low = 0, high = nums.length - 1;\n    while (low < high) {\n        int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;\n        if (nums[mid] > nums[mid + 1]) high = mid;   // downhill: a peak is at mid or to its left\n        else low = mid + 1;                          // uphill: a peak is to the right\n    }\n    return low;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "int findPeakElement(vector<int>& nums) {\n    int low = 0, high = nums.size() - 1;\n    while (low < high) {\n        int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;\n        if (nums[mid] > nums[mid + 1]) high = mid;   // downhill: a peak is at mid or to its left\n        else low = mid + 1;                          // uphill: a peak is to the right\n    }\n    return low;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(log n), space O(1).** The range `low..high` always contains a peak, and it shrinks every step, so when one index is left, it's a peak. (In JavaScript, `(low + high) / 2` is safe; in Java and C++ use `low + (high - low) / 2`.)"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Comparing the approaches"
    },
    {
      "type": "complexity",
      "rows": [
        {
          "approach": "Linear scan",
          "time": "O(n)",
          "timeWhy": "It walks until the **first downhill step**, which may be the end.",
          "space": "O(1)",
          "spaceWhy": "Just an index."
        },
        {
          "approach": "Binary search toward the higher side",
          "time": "O(log n)",
          "timeWhy": "The range always holds a peak and **halves** every step.",
          "space": "O(1)",
          "spaceWhy": "Only `low` and `high`."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Edge cases to test"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**One value** — index 0 is a peak.",
        "**Always rising / always falling** — the last / first index.",
        "**Several peaks** — any one of them is a correct answer, so test by checking the returned index is a peak, not by comparing with one expected index.",
        "**Extreme values** — `[-2147483648]` or values next to `INT_MIN`: compare neighbours, never add them."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Variations"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**Peak Index in a Mountain Array** — exactly one peak (previous lesson).",
        "**Find a Peak Element II** — a peak in a 2D grid: binary search the columns, taking each column's maximum, in O(m log n).",
        "**Find Minimum in Rotated Sorted Array** — the same \"which side is safe?\" reasoning on a rotated array (Pattern 3)."
      ]
    }
